    <h2>The Fence Calls Are Leaking Out of Town</h2>

    <p>Trace where a Cullman fence lead begins. A family on a town lot wants privacy fence up before the dog arrives and searches "privacy fence Cullman AL." A cattleman out toward Fairview needs a long run of woven wire and equipment-width gates and types "farm fence company near me." A Smith Lake owner wants a fence that works with a sloped, setback-bound lot and looks for someone who's fenced the shoreline before. Each is a ready buyer with a very different job. But most fence companies here run a single-page site with a photo and a number — nothing that tells a search engine whether you brace cattle corners or finish a clean privacy line — so an out-of-county company's service-area page wins the click by default, and the rancher can't tell the pasture specialist from the backyard crew. The work is here, spread from town lots to pasture to shoreline, and it's leaking out of the county.</p>

    <h2>Your Read on Cullman's Fence Markets Is an Edge Nobody Out of Town Can Fake</h2>

    <p>The first question a fence company that knows Cullman asks isn't what fence you want — it's which of three jobs this is, and an out-of-county crew almost never asks it. Town privacy fence is about height, materials, and a clean line on a small lot. Farm and livestock fence is a different trade entirely: thousands of feet of woven wire, high-tensile, or barbed run across the pasture country around Fairview and Vinemont, engineered around corner bracing and tension, with gates wide enough for equipment and layouts that actually keep cattle where they belong. Lake fence at Smith Lake works around slopes and shoreline setbacks a flat-lot crew never plans for. A company set up for suburban privacy fence isn't equipped to brace a mile of cattle fence, and a farm-fence outfit may not do the fine finish a backyard wants — matching the company to the job is half of it. Underneath all three sits the same detail that decides whether a fence stands: the post. It has to go 24 to 36 inches into firm ground, spaced six to eight feet on residential runs, in a concrete footing crowned to shed water away from the post rather than a cup that traps it — because a post sitting in held water rots at grade and works loose, and that, not the dirt, is what leans a fence by its second summer. On livestock fence the whole load lives in the corners and H-braces, which carry the wire tension, and that's exactly where a rushed crew saves time it shouldn't. Reading which job it is, and setting the post right for it, is an edge a windshield estimate can't touch.</p>

